[low] policies/elbv2_target_health_state_test.rego:34 (test quality)
The tests cover healthy, unhealthy, exceptions, draining, and type skipping, but they never exercise the unavailable branch controlled by data.treat_unavailable_as_failure. That behavior is an explicit policy contract, so a regression that makes unavailable always fail or never fail would still pass the current 5/5 tests.
Suggested fix: Add tests proving unavailable is non-failing by default, fails when data.treat_unavailable_as_failure is true, and is suppressed when the target ID is allowed.
Guidance: In policies/elbv2_target_health_state_test.rego, add focused tests for the existing unavailable branch in compliance_framework.elbv2_target_health_state. Add one test showing `count(policy.violation) == 0` for `target_health_input("unavailable")` with default data, one test showing `count(policy.violation) == 1` for the same input when overriding `data.treat_unavailable_as_failure` to true, and one test showing `count(policy.violation) == 0` when both `data.treat_unavailable_as_failure` is true and `data.allowed_unhealthy_target_ids` contains `i-0123456789abcdef0`. Use flat top-level data overrides with `with data.treat_unavailable_as_failure as true` and `with data.allowed_unhealthy_target_ids as [...]`; do not introduce `input.policy_inputs`, because tunables are deliberately read from top-level data.
